## 00934 PATIENT=1 DOCTOR=2
1: Hello, doctor! I've had psoriasis for years, but now my joints are hurting, and my nails look terrible. I thought it was time to come in. ~ cc s1 s2
2: Good to meet you. Tell me about the joints first. Which ones? ~ s1
1: Mainly my fingers, and my right knee. They're stiff and swollen. ~ s1
2: When are they worst? ~ s1
1: In the morning. It takes me a good hour to get going. ~ s1
2: How long has this been happening? ~ s1
1: About four months. It started slowly. ~ s1
2: And the nails? ~ s2
1: They're pitted, little dents, and a couple of them have come away from the nail bed. ~ s2
2: Any eye redness or pain, or back pain at night? ~ n1
1: No. Not that I've noticed. ~ n1
2: What do you use for the psoriasis? ~ m1
1: A steroid cream on my elbows, calcipotriol, once a day. ~ m1
2: Do you take anything for the joint pain? ~
1: Just ibuprofen, 400 mg, when it's bad. ~ m2
2: Any other conditions? ~ h1
1: Psoriasis since I was twenty. That's all. ~ h1
2: Does psoriasis or arthritis run in the family? ~
1: My father had psoriasis. ~
2: Do you smoke, or drink alcohol? ~ so1
1: I smoke ten a day. I'd like to cut down. ~ so1
2: Let me check your blood pressure and pulse. ~
1: Go on. ~ pause=10
2: Blood pressure 126 over 80, pulse 72, temperature 36.6. ~ v
1: Normal? ~
2: Normal. Now let me look at your hands and your knee. ~
1: Of course. ~ pause=18
2: There's swelling and tenderness in several finger joints, and the nails are pitted with lifting. The right knee has a small effusion. ~ x1
1: What does that mean? ~
2: It strongly suggests psoriatic arthritis, and the nail changes support it. I'd also like blood tests and X-rays of your hands. ~ d1 p1
1: Is it serious? ~
2: It can damage joints if it is not treated, so early treatment is important. ~
1: What treatment? ~
2: There are several options, and I'd like a rheumatologist to guide it. I'll refer you urgently, and in the meantime take naproxen, 500 mg, twice a day with food. ~ m3 f1
1: All right. ~
2: And it would help your joints and skin to stop smoking. I can help you with that. ~ c1
1: I'd like that. ~
2: I'll see you in four weeks, once the tests are back. ~ f2
1: Thank you, doctor. ~
@@
