## 00938 PATIENT=1 DOCTOR=2 NURSE=3
1: Um, sorry, I've got these blisters on my chest, and they're very painful. I think it might be shingles, but I'm not sure. ~ cc s1
2: Let's have a look at the story. Where are they, exactly? ~ s1
1: On the right side of my chest, in a band, round to my back. ~ s1
2: When did you first notice them? ~ s1 u1
1: Four days ago? Maybe five? I think it started with a tingling. ~ s1 u1
3: In the triage notes you said Tuesday, so that's four days. ~ s1 u1
1: Yes, Tuesday, I suppose. ~ s1 u1
2: Is it painful or itchy? ~ s2
1: Burning, mostly. It's worse at night. ~ s2
2: Any fever, or feeling unwell? ~ n1
1: A bit achy, but no fever. ~ n1
2: Any blisters near your eyes, or your face? ~ n2
1: No. Only on the chest. ~ n2
2: Have you had chickenpox in the past? ~ h1
1: As a child, yes. ~ h1
2: Do you have any allergies to medicines? ~ a1
1: No, I don't have any allergies. ~ a1
3: Doctor, I've done his observations. Pulse 78, blood pressure 130 over 82, temperature 36.9, oxygen 98 percent, breathing 16. ~ v
2: Thank you. Let me look at the rash. Please lift your shirt. ~
1: Of course. ~ pause=16
2: Clusters of blisters on a red base, in a band on the right chest wall, not crossing the middle. That is classic for shingles. ~ d1
1: So it is shingles? ~
2: Yes. Because you're within the first few days, I'd like to start an antiviral, aciclovir, 800 mg, five times a day for seven days. ~ p1 p2
1: Five times a day? ~
2: Yes, about every four hours while you're awake. And simple painkillers for the pain. ~
1: Is it contagious? ~
2: To anyone who hasn't had chickenpox, yes, until the blisters crust over. Please avoid pregnant women, babies and anyone with a weak immune system. ~ c1
3: I'll give you a leaflet on that. ~
2: I'd like to see you again in a week, to check how it's healing. ~ f1
1: Thank you, doctor. ~
@@
